(()=>{
'use strict';
const $=q=>document.querySelector(q);
function css(){if($('#pcConclusionCss'))return;const s=document.createElement('style');s.id='pcConclusionCss';s.textContent=`
#whatWeLearned{scroll-margin-top:80px}.pc-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px;margin:18px 0}.pc-card{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:14px;background:linear-gradient(180deg,var(--panel2),var(--panel));padding:16px}.pc-card h3{margin:.1rem 0 .35rem;font-size:1.05rem}.pc-kicker{font-size:.75rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.09em;color:var(--cyan);font-weight:700}.pc-flow{display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;margin:14px 0}.pc-flow span{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:999px;padding:7px 10px;background:#111621}.pc-arrow{color:var(--cyan)}.pc-tests{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:10px;margin-top:14px}.pc-test{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:13px;background:#10141d}.pc-test b{display:block;margin-bottom:4px}.pc-good{border-left:4px solid var(--good)}.pc-warn{border-left:4px solid var(--warn)}.pc-bad{border-left:4px solid var(--bad)}@media(max-width:850px){.pc-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.pc-tests{grid-template-columns:1fr}}`;document.head.appendChild(s)}
function build(){if($('#whatWeLearned'))return true;const status=$('#status');if(!status)return false;css();const sec=document.createElement('section');sec.className='section wrap';sec.id='whatWeLearned';sec.innerHTML=`
<h2>What the tests have taught us</h2>
<p class="lead">We started with a broad idea: perhaps the 3×3, 4×4 and 5×5 magic squares could act as a bridge between written language, sound and number. The tests narrowed that idea substantially. The strongest evidence is now about <b>phonetic geometry</b> — how sounds are arranged by articulation — rather than alphabet order or powers of three.</p>
<div class="pc-flow" aria-label="Current best-supported model"><span><b>3×3</b> coarse sound regions</span><span class="pc-arrow">→</span><span><b>4×4</b> contrast / bridge layer</span><span class="pc-arrow">→</span><span><b>5×5</b> finer articulatory organization</span></div>
<div class="pc-grid">
  <div class="pc-card"><div class="pc-kicker">Strongest result</div><h3>Phonetic coordinates beat alphabet order</h3><p>When letters were numbered by writing-system order, the model performed badly. When the numbers came from phonetic coordinates instead, the direction reversed and the result replicated on held-out WikiPron datasets. That points to <b>sound geometry, not alphabet sequence</b>, as the useful variable.</p></div>
  <div class="pc-card"><div class="pc-kicker">Mechanism</div><h3>Locality does most of the work</h3><p>The ablation test separated the score into spatial locality, adjacency and powers-of-3 spacing. Spatial locality carried the clearest positive signal. The powers-of-3 term was negative overall, so it is kept as an exploratory overlay rather than treated as the cause of the effect.</p></div>
  <div class="pc-card"><div class="pc-kicker">4×4</div><h3>Dürer behaves like an opposition bridge</h3><p>The Dürer complement pairs are unusually far apart in articulatory feature space. In a direct antipode test, the numerical complement was the exact nearest predicted phonetic opposite for 6 of 16 cells, in the top three for 11 of 16, and far better than random pairing.</p></div>
  <div class="pc-card"><div class="pc-kicker">5×5</div><h3>Siamese structure is unusually compatible</h3><p>The standard Siamese 5×5 arrangement outperformed most other valid 5×5 magic-square variants on phonetic locality. Its advantage was especially strong for consonant structure and place/manner relationships, while pure vowel-to-vowel movement often worked against the square.</p></div>
  <div class="pc-card"><div class="pc-kicker">Interaction</div><h3>No single property explains it</h3><p>Magic sums alone, complement geometry alone, vowel/consonant placement alone and place/manner compactness alone were not enough. In 5×5, <b>magic + complement geometry</b> recovered most of the canonical advantage, and adding place/manner structure recovered still more.</p></div>
  <div class="pc-card"><div class="pc-kicker">Boundary</div><h3>Interesting does not mean universal</h3><p>Phonetic-optimized non-magic layouts can still outperform the canonical squares. The present evidence therefore supports a non-random relationship between these squares and phonetic geometry, not a claim that the squares are uniquely optimal or that all languages must follow them.</p></div>
</div>
<h3>How we tested it</h3>
<p class="small">The tests below were run directly against pinned local WikiPron and PHOIBLE data. They were not processed through GitHub. Each test asks a different question so that one positive result cannot do all the work.</p>
<div class="pc-tests">
  <div class="pc-test pc-good"><b>1. Full coordinate benchmark</b>Mapped real WikiPron phone transitions into 3×3, 4×4 and 5×5 PHOIBLE-derived coordinate grids, applied the fixed Lo Shu / Dürer / Siamese permutations, and compared them with randomized mappings.</div>
  <div class="pc-test pc-good"><b>2. Frozen hold-out validation</b>Kept the method unchanged and applied it to 195 previously unused WikiPron datasets. The positive direction strengthened, showing the result was not limited to the original canonical dataset.</div>
  <div class="pc-test pc-good"><b>3. Component ablation</b>Separated spatial locality, adjacency and powers-of-3 spacing, and tested 3×3, 4×4 and 5×5 independently. This showed that locality is the strongest contributor and powers-of-3 spacing is not.</div>
  <div class="pc-test pc-good"><b>4. Vowel/consonant analysis</b>Split transitions into vowel→vowel, consonant→consonant and vowel↔consonant. The positive effect was concentrated mainly in consonant and cross-category structure, not pure vowel→vowel movement.</div>
  <div class="pc-test pc-good"><b>5. Alphabet V/C and hybrid tests</b>Tested whether natural vowel/consonant placement in writing systems could replace phonetic coordinates. It produced a measurable 3×3 signal, but the all-coordinate model was still clearly better.</div>
  <div class="pc-test pc-good"><b>6. Magic-square specificity</b>Compared the canonical squares with other valid magic squares, rotations/reflections, random layouts and train-optimized non-magic layouts. Dürer and especially Siamese were unusually good among magic variants, but not globally optimal.</div>
  <div class="pc-test pc-good"><b>7. Property destruction + factorial test</b>Preserved or destroyed magic sums, complement geometry, V/C boundaries and place/manner compactness one at a time, then in combinations. This isolated the strongest 5×5 interaction as magic + complement + articulation.</div>
  <div class="pc-test pc-good"><b>8. Complement and antipode tests</b>Checked whether complement pairs were simply common transitions (they were not), then inverted PHOIBLE feature vectors and asked whether Dürer complements matched the predicted articulatory opposites. They did far above chance.</div>
</div>
<div class="notice"><b>Current interpretation:</b> 3×3 is best treated as a coarse phonetic layer, 4×4 as an articulatory contrast/bridge layer, and 5×5 as a finer organization layer in which magic/complement structure matters strongly. The powers-of-3 overlay remains mathematically interesting but is <b>not supported as the mechanism producing the phonetic effect</b>.</div>
`;
status.parentNode.insertBefore(sec,status);return true}
if(!build()){const o=new MutationObserver(()=>{if(build())o.disconnect()});o.observe(document.documentElement,{childList:true,subtree:true});setTimeout(()=>o.disconnect(),10000)}
})();